A||||40||theogre||Mirror turn signal project||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||02-02-2000||08:07 PM||||Over in general someone asked about what mods to make.... There was a note about mirror turn signals. ((I didn't think anyone wanted these but me.... I should have known with this bunch.))<P>I have a way to do this that puts a line of light on the bottom of the mirror shell. The light is synced to the dash indicator. I'm working out the construction details now. I know how I want to wire it, but I need to work out some structural stuff.<P>Does anyone have a mirror, even with a broken glass, from either side of the car that they don't want and can drop in the mail? In return that person gets the first published copy of the construction details. The only extra mirrod I have has a busted shell. I don't want to try this directly on the car first time.<P>The light I'm building will attach to the interior side of the edge of the shell, and not the glass. I looked at the glass and making it show thru the glass would require modification of both the glass and the mounting tray it sits in. <P>It could be done thru the glass, but it's allot of work. Would also need a printed circuit to hold the LED's. The way I'm doing needs only basic soldering skills and little interior wiring.<P>I only did a little looking at sources for the parts. looks like posible color choices will be green, red, orange or yellow, and maybe blue. Total parts cost depends on exactly where I get the parts, and what color(the blue ones are really expencive), but it looks to be around a 20-30$us project to do both sides.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sage||02-02-2000||08:42 PM||||Yes I have a passenger side mirror with a broken glass in it.  You are welcome to it. Just let me know what you need done with it.  You can email me, or just answer here, whichever you prefer.  Am not really interested at present in doing this mod myself, but am happy to help out if possible.<p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 02-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ITA_FIERO||02-02-2000||09:40 PM||||I have several mirrors as well.  Both driver's side (broken glass) and passenger side (2, 1 no glass, 1 intact).  Not the greatest looking...but structurally sound.  LMK||||reg||1
Z||000003||TwisT||02-03-2000||01:58 AM||||Just a note here...<P>'99 and '00 Honda CBR 1100XX Blackbird (sportbike) has rear-view mirrors which incorporate amber turn-signals.  I've seen these units used on cars before (the whole mirror) and it looks pretty slick.  Just mount,  wire and you're done.  Just a thought...<p>[This message has been edited by TwisT (edited 02-03-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-03-2000||03:28 AM||||ogre--I don't have any mirrors to send you (sorry), but I'd be very interested in the finished project.  Will you be selling a kit or just instructions?  If just the instructions, how much do you plan on charging?  Any idea yet.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||02-03-2000||09:22 AM||||The thing will probably just be instrutcions with part sources, etc. I don't know what, if anything, I'll charge for the information, I'm not planning to make a kit. Depends on how much time it takes to make the second one(the first one always takes longer than it should). That will give me a better idea of the dificulty. If I made a kit it would likely only be the assembled LED lamp. You'd have to glue it in. Right now I'm trying to determin the best type of LED's and brightness. To bright and your mirror won't be much use at night with them on. to dim and they won't show durring the day. It's going to take me ahwile to work that part out.<P>The project is not very dificult if you can solder properly. You have to be clean with the iron or you make a mess that could fail on you after installing it.<BR> <BR>the tricky part looks like instalation. Agian it's not hard, but if you don't get it right it will be messy and you'll likely have to start over. I'm planning to set the assembled lamp with epoxy to protect it from weather, car washing, etc. There's really no way other than epoxy that I can see. Space is pretty tight, and it has to be really tough. Maybe black RTV, but RTV doesn't stick to some things well. We'll see. That's why I'll need a test mirror.<P>I'll let you know where I need test mirrors sent. Thanks guys.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88 Fiero||02-03-2000||11:10 AM||||That's so cool Ogre...Let us know what's the outcome...I am sure alot of members would be interested!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||lawrence||02-03-2000||12:36 PM||||Some comments to theogre on the LED's -- <P>I would stay away from blue.  I think the Police are the only ones allowed to have blue lights on a car.<P>I would get the brightest LED's available, then dim them when you turn on the headlights.  You could do this with a chopper (like a lamp dimmer), a regulator, or maybe even a different resistor.<P>Are you doing this for you to see the indicator or for other drivers to better see that you are turning?  If it's the latter, I would try to mount the LED as far outside as possible.  Did you consider a regular lamp?  Maybe you could mount one behind the glass and paint the inside of the shell silver.  Then the entire outer ring between the glass and the shell would light up.<P>However you do it, you should run an extra pair of wires to the mirror while you have the door apart.  This way you can add a down lamp that lights up the ground when the door opens.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||02-03-2000||02:55 PM||||what color light seems mostly restricted by state, I had forgotten about that one. It usually applies to emergency lights on the roof or facing forward. I wasn't going to mess with blue anyway. I looked at them a bit more and they are bloody expencive, around a dollar(US) each. They are also picky about ESD which might make them not last well in an exterior use like this. (the face of the LEDs will be exposed the rest of the body and all wiring will be burried in epoxy when finished.)<P>Right now I'm looking at rectangular LED's and maybe non difused super bright red ones. The super brights are often used in the lights in wings now so I know they will work well for this. I'm thinking the rectangles, which look better but are available only in difused, won't be bright enough.<P>The purpose is as an aux signal for drivers who are part way along side already.<P>I had thought about a lamp behind the glass, there's enough room to be sure, but I think it will throw to much glair. One of my main conditions is that the mirror remain fully functional at all times. With LED's, especially clear capsule ones, nearly all the light is focused in a fairly narrow direction.<P>I was thinking about a dimmer. I think the super brights may be focused enough to not be a problem. I'll have to see with one of the prototypes.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DRH||02-03-2000||03:48 PM||||Ever see the "turn signals" on one of the really old VW Beetles? They had little metal flags that dropped down out of the posts behind the doors.<P>Sorry Ogre... Sounds like a worthwhile project. That image just popped in my head as I was reading about visibility from the side...<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 02-03-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000010||TOM||02-03-2000||09:07 PM||||They are called semiphores||||reg||1
