Z||000012||batboy||02-04-2000||07:30 AM||||Yep, I knew about them too, and yes I'm old, but not too dusty, I bathe once a week whether I need it or not. My first car was a 1956 VW. Now this had regular turn signals, but I had lots of VW books and magazines at the time and learned all about the VWs and their history. I even made my old Volks into a baja bug. I started customing and hotrodding cars at a ripe old age of 16 back in the early 70's. I had Okraska heads, dual carbs, and tuned exhaust on that little engine. I could beat all the other VWs, but not much else (maybe a Corvair or two).||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||02-04-2000||04:09 PM||||I had a '75 Super that the previous owner had put a 21xx cc motor in.  That little car would scream!  I also found out the hard way that they float.  I always thought it was a rumor.||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||02-04-2000||07:25 PM||||anyway, back to the topic,<P>I figured out a good way to wire the LEDs so that if one fails it won't be hugely noticable. (A series/parrallel lattice for those that might care. you have to kill both leds in a pair to kill the whole set. Since 20 leds fit across I'm using 2 10 led sets.)<P>Also figured out what LEDs to use. Will be 3mm ultra bright round reds with clear shells. they will be nearly invisible unless lit. Space below the mirror is a bit tight tho, I'll have to decide wether to grind a bit off the mirror shell, or off the mirror mounting tray. If you put them up the side there's more room but I like the bottom better so far. So that's where the first prototype will have them.<P>Some one check my math, I haven't done this in years.<P>I need 40mA at 14V. 14/.4=35ohms for a resitor. (The test LEDs i'm using at the moment are using a 67 ohm resitor to get around 18ma at 12v and I can see the 3 of those at 50 feet. I left them on for 2 hours like that and didn't fry them so I guess my decimal is in the right place.) I figured it at 14V since the system runs 13-15.5 for it's normal range. (at 16 volts it's a 40 ohm resistor, anyone think I should go higher than that for safety? 40ma drive isn't even absolute max yet.)<P>I would have liked to use 2x5mm rectangle LEDs but they don't make them in clear shell or superbright that I can find. The difuser ones loose to much light out the sides. and they are only 1mcd to start with. The super brights are 80+mcd. ((20 of them is 1.6+ candles. It's bright.))At 30feet the 3mm round ones pretty much become a solid line tho. Just would have looked better up close. At least the water clear shells will vannish almost completely. Even as bright as the 3mm ones are they put it all into a 45 degree cone. that means no mirror glare. I'll likely angle them away from the car body a few degrees to make the viewing andle as wide as posible. no sence wasting half the light on the side of the car.<P>Also found the LEDs for under 30$us per hundred. That was nice since the final run will use 40 of them to do both sides.<P>May also have found another posiblilty for LEDs...... Increasing the brightness of the side markers with no increase in heat.<P>Nice thing about LEDs is that current draw is small for the light you get. I won't be suprised if the stock turn signal can never notices the increase.||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||02-04-2000||07:44 PM||||How about a brake light that mounts in an '84,pace car scoop to go with it?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Formula||02-04-2000||08:53 PM||||Is the LED reflecting off the mirror back? or is it going straight back?<P>And how hard is it to pull the door panels off to run the wires?<P>This sounds like a really nifty idea. I think you should rub some dialkyldithiophosphate (word of the day) on the mirror to make it rotate smoother.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mark||02-04-2000||09:15 PM||||Some guidelines on calculating the value of the current limiting resistor......<P>The voltage across the resistor is equal to the supply voltage minus the total voltage drop across all series-connected LEDs.  The current through the resistor is equal to the voltage across the resistor divided by the resistor value.  Or conversely, <P>        R = Vr / I<P>As an example, for a supply voltage of 14 VDC, a desired current of 40 mA (= 0.04 A), and with a typical forward voltage drop of 1.2 volts across each of two series-connected LEDs, the proper resistor value is  (14-1.2-1.2)/0.04 = 290 ohm.  The power dissipated in this resistor is P=I xI x R, or 0.04 x 0.04 x 290= 0.464 watt.  <P>Note that the current will increase (and so will the power dissipated in the resistor) when the voltage source goes above 14 vdc.<P>You can reduce the power dissipated in the resistor (and also your overall parts count) by connecting more LEDs in series. Remember, a 1/2 watt resistor will get HOT when it is dissipating rated power. <P>Finally, don't worry about burning out the LEDs if you don't exceed the recommended maximum forward current.||||reg||1
Z||000018||lawrence||02-04-2000||09:24 PM||||40 mills is .04, not .4<P>Hosfelt Electronics has red LEDs with 15,000 mcd, orange LEDs (would be my color choice) at 18,000 and yellow LEDs at 23,000.  They operate at 2 volts, so you can probably put 6-7 of them in series without any resistor.<P>They're expensive ($3.49) but then you might not need as many.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.hosfelt.com/Leds/led1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hosfelt.com/Leds/led1.htm</A> <P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Mark||02-04-2000||09:59 PM||||Lawrence, you must use a current limiting resistor.  It's the only thing that will keep the LED current from going through the roof when the voltage fluctuates above nominal.<P>In general, you should select your resistor to limit LED current to the maximum rating (or less)while at the highest supply voltage and assuming a low-tolerance resistor.||||reg||1
Z||000020||theogre||02-05-2000||01:54 PM||||Thanks guys. I knew my math was hosed. God knows where my old books are.<P>The LEDs I'm using probably LiteOn ones from digikey. they are under 25$us per hundred. I could easilly see 19mcd LEDs across a very bright room (4 quad tube floresent in a room that about 15x15) and they where just sitting on the off white desk. I could see them well at over 50 feet and I only had 3 on the test set. The 80mcd reds should work fine. The total output of 20 of them is 1600. that's more than allot of small lightbulbs.<P>The LEDs will point aft out from the mirror shell along the bottom edge. They focus their light into a 45 degree cone. If I aim them away from the body a little then the viewing angle will be quite wide. Maybe up a bit to the truckers see tham better too. I could use fewer, and brighter, ones but I wanted the size 20 wide gives. 20 will light a line from the outboard corner all the way to the pedastal.<P>As for a brake light, yes it can be done. You could put it anyplace you want that you can hide the wires. They are commonly used for third brakes on wings. The hard part, most of the time, is figuring out how to mount them.<P>Hmmmmm..... the sun came out. I just tried the 3 20mcd ones. You can see them out side, but not well..... I think I'll test a few 80's and see how they are before I order 100. I guess I'll have to get one or 2 from scaggin radio shack, if they even have any.<P>The reason for my wiring them in a latice is that you can fail up to 5 leds in a set, as long as they aren't a pair, and the other 5 still light. I could make it even more redundant. I could make it so that in a set of 20 you could fail all the way down to only 5. You'd about have to blow the mirror off the car to fail 5 let alone 15. Of course the latice made the math more interesting. parralleling 2 LEDs doesn't hurt brightness so you'd notice. paralleling 4 makes them dim unless you up the current. problem is uping the current to much makes the rest of the group fry if you blow one or two. With this latic if you treat it like a single series group, when one in the pair fails the other one still lights and is only slightly brighter. The rest are unaffected.<P>I thought about orange and other collors. You can do all sorts of things. I'm going to use red mostly because it's what I ran into first. I'll have a look at the others and the price. I was trying to keep cost down. but it the others work better, they are likely worth the cost. They would definatlye work for a side marker where the number of them used would be smaller. Those dim OEM markers are annoying. I can't belive they got them thru DOT testing. Even at night you hardly see them. The fact that the front ones are part of the turn signals makes them even more inane.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||87 Steve GT||02-05-2000||03:58 PM||||And putting some sort of bulb ((behind)) the mirror would give a glair???  Could'nt you just wire it to light up only when the front oem sidemarker lites up??  How do you think that it would look??  Just wondering??  thank's theorge for making everyone really think as a group....   Steve||||reg||5
Z||000022||lawrence||02-05-2000||09:21 PM||||Mark,<P>Resistors are not needed for LEDs.  The only reason you would use one is to reduce the voltage.  If you put 2 volts on a single LED without any resistor, it would be quite happy.<P>I built a microprocessor simulator that has 16 indicator LEDs on it.  Rather than put 16 resistors up to 5 volts, I put in a 2.0 volt regulator and tied the LEDs directly to it.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A> <p>[This message has been edited by lawrence (edited 02-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||fiero56||02-05-2000||10:26 PM||||Have any of you guys seen the brake lights on public busses lately? They use a cluster of red LEDs behind a circular lens. They are very bright even durring the day. Also, in bicycle lighting, NiteRider makes a very bright LED rear light(12v even). That thing will blind you even in broad daylight. I'm not sure who the LEDs are by or what they cost, but might be worth pursuing something a little brighter if you wanted.||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||02-06-2000||01:20 PM||||adding a lamp to the side marker would likely change the flash rate of the signals, would also be on when you put on the parking lights wich is not desirable in this application. <P>Putting an unfocused light source in/on the mirror would throw light on the door glass giving the same result as headlights/sun on the windshield. I.E. it would tend to wash out the mirror at night making it harder to see.<P>As mark reminded me, you don't need current or voltage regulation in the forward direction. You only need that if the LEDs ever see reversed voltage. The only way these LEDs would see reverse polarity is if you connected the battery backward, and turned on the key, and turned on the turn signal. By then you'd damage allot more important things than the LEDs.<P>As bright as LEDs can be they draw hardly any current. So they shouldn't change the flash rate much it at all. Plus the clear shell LEDs are focused. you can easuly control the direction the project in.<P>I've seen those new brake lights one things. Very common on all new comercial duty trucks, not just municipal buses. very nice they are I think. You'd have to totally smash it for it not to work.<P>I looked at the hosfelt site.... they don't have much in 3mm. The really bright ones are all 5mm or larger. They won't fit. They did have other colors of 3mm besides red but they are about the same brightness. Does anyone have their print catalog to look and see what 3mm they have and how bright.||||reg||1
Z||000025||lawrence||02-06-2000||02:00 PM||||theogre,<P>I have a Hosfelt catalog.  They list several 3mm LEDs.  I'll only list the ones that are brighter than 80, since you've already found that source (too bad this isn't fixed font):<P>mcd      color       price    lens<BR>1000     White       $3.49    water clear<BR> 250     Blue         1.99    water clear<BR> 300     Red           .12    water clear<BR> 120     Green         .12    green clear<BR> 200     Green         .24    green clear<BR> 100     Green         .18    colorless<BR>  89     Yellow        .18    colorless<P>I'm not sure if the 'green clear' would give you the punch you want, but it doesn't say diffused, either.<P>BTW, are you going to sequence these  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||Monkeyman||02-06-2000||05:44 PM||||fiero56/ogre--For what it's worth, you can find LED tail lights at truck stops all day long.  I realize this isn't what you're looking for, but you might be able to do some research and find some other sources of LEDs.  I remember that Grote makes some of the LED tail lights.||||reg||1
Z||000027||87 Steve GT||02-07-2000||07:38 AM||||Well yesterday I finally took both mirror's off((it's ok because I have a wink mrror))  For those who don't know what a wink mirror is.... it's a 5 panel rearview mirror that goes all the way across the car... can see all with one....  anyway.... on the back of the side mirror themselves are 4 small holes that you can see light through to the front side... I'm going to try to make it show through the mirror... It may or may not work... I'll let you all know asap....  steve||||reg||3
Z||000028||theogre||02-07-2000||09:06 AM||||Lawrence,<P>Thanks. I think your right, normally "green clear" would mean non difused. just green. They would be as bright, or very close to it, as water clear. they would just be green when off. I'll have to get their catalog I guess, Digikey doesn't have anything that bright listed.<P>Steve,<P>The outside mirrors should be what are called "second surface" mirrors. The coating is on the back of the glass. You'll probably have to remove the coating. It can be done but it's a pain to do neatly. even harder w/o taking the glass off the mounting tray.<P>Another problem with LEDs anywhere on the glass/tray is that the LEDs move with the glass. The glass is often tiped toward the car. With the LEDs on th shell you can set the aim and they are always going to be pointing in a direction to do the most good. One of the things I've noticed with the OEM arrow in the mirror is that it's really good one from behind the vehicle. Once you change lanes it's pretty dim. if you are pulling along side it's even dimmer, even at night. I want this to be something that increases the hight and width ofthe signal systems visibility. It's especially important on a black Fiero which literally disappears under some of the bigger over passes around here.<P>Monkey,<P>Thanks, The people who make those lights have to buy similar LEDs as we do for this project, it was a good idea. I know who makes the LEDs. (there aren't that many makers) problem is finding a distributor that sells in small quantities. The people who make the tail lights buy them 10-100,000 at a time. I want at most maybe 100-1000. Even when you have a distributor, like Digikey, they don't always have what you want. ((Kind of like the local car parts store.))||||reg||1
Z||000029||theogre||02-07-2000||07:21 PM||||Well, since I've got most of the wiring worked out I started writing the instructions for that much. Now all I need to do is get enough LED's to make a side and mount it. That part may not happen till the weather is warmer. ||||reg||1
Z||000030||87 Steve GT||02-07-2000||10:50 PM||||theorge,<P>    Do you have any idea how to remove the *second surface* ??  Just scratch it off?? I dont want to damage the mirror at all. I found some pretty flat LED's @ *yucko* Radio Shack... As far as the LED's being on the tray itself I think they will fit  and be able to put them there safely..lol<P> pointing them toward the outside of the car would be the brightest...  Right???  <P>This is fun... I bought my 87GT about 6 month ago... I can't remember what i did with my time before my Fiero...  lol  <BR>                       thank's Steve,Tyler,Tx||||reg||1
Z||000031||theogre||02-08-2000||10:03 AM||||Steve, that's kind of an oxy morron isn't it? the coating is what makes the mirror work. It's likely if you have to remove the mirror from the tray it will break or damage the coating. Otherwise at the least you'll have to scrape the coating, which in reallity damages the mirror. Either way if your experiment doesn't work you'll have to replace the glass.<P>There are a number of LED's, mostly surface mount type, that could be used that way.<P>As I think I said before, one of the things I don't like about putting them on the glass is that the aim changes every time you move the mirror. I think even initial aiming will be annoying to very diffictult.||||reg||1
Z||000032||theogre||02-08-2000||08:05 PM||||Brighter yet!!!!<P>JDR has these:<BR>LED, Red Super Bright 1400MCD 3MM (T1) LED152 $.39ea.<P>combined output from 20 of them is 28 candelas. That's getting up with the actual brake light filiment brightness. It's brighter than the taillight filament which is about 10candles I think. Think we'll see those in daylight?||||reg||1
