Z||000035||theogre||02-09-2000||06:43 PM||||The LED's I listed above are over 20$us. You need otherthings after that. This is not going to be an easy project.||||reg||1
Z||000036||theogre||02-10-2000||04:01 PM||||Boy did this just become a pain in the rear....... I just tried a 3000mcd LED I picked up radish rack durring lunch. 3000...... and you still can hardly see it. That's more than twice the brightest 3mm LED's I can find. <P>Hosfelt's got Toshiba 8000mcd ones. Problem is all the really bright ones are 5mm (T-1 3/4) shells or even bigger. There's not 5mm of space between the mirror and the shell. <P>Joy, not.||||reg||1
Z||000037||theogre||02-10-2000||08:30 PM||||Well,,,, I checked a few things.<P>LED's need current limiters even in the forward direction under certain conditions. They get really warm if they want a resistor and there's not one there.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Even running off a pair of pen light cells I got warmed by one red one I was testing. <P>(I thought that sounded odd, but most of the time I run LED's off a pen light battery or a single in a computer indicator. I forgot the indicators for motherboard functions are limited on the CPU board. The math for the LED's was right....)<P>The 3000 mcd is not only not bright enough it's projected light is so narrow it's nearly useless. This looks like a problem with nearly all the high brightness ones.<P>I also just tested a orange 1600mcd 5mm. It's bright but same problem... to narrow.<P>For the giggles of it I got one of these 10mm red-orange 12000mcd (12 candelas)ones from radish rack. (276-206) This thing is bright. throws light on the wall from 20 feet away and has a big pattern. Problem is it's 10mm. It only will fit in the outside bottom corner and even then requires alteration. I think a pair of these in the corner may do what I want. It will be at least a few days before I can fool with it much more for fit. I'll check the daytime viewing tomorow.<P>The original idea I was working with is nearing death. I can maybe get it bright enough but I don't think I can get the light pattern I want. I'll play with some more LED's over the next week or so but it's not looking good. MY original idea would still be good for a third brake light or something that doesn't need the viewing angle the mirror signal needs.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||Sage||02-10-2000||11:07 PM||||Bulb and fiber-optic cable? Just a thought. ||||reg||1
Z||000039||theogre||02-11-2000||09:12 AM||||Sage, I had thought of that. problem is fiber is expencive and it doesn't realy make this any eazier. You have to build the fiber driver, and then figure out how to anchore the cable.... most of the fiber I've seen is jacketed in materials that don't bond well with many adhesives. <P>Steve, While I had the mirror out last night I noticed 2 holes in the mounting tray and that the glass was reflective on the back instead of dull. (dull would mean a second surface mirror with backing paint.) For the giggles of it I held it up to light and the light came thru. Then I tested it with a LED. You lose about 1/2 the light comming thru but it does come thru. I don't know if all the mirrors are like this. IF they are you'd have to carefully mill an opening into the tray to let your ligths show thru. you wouldn't want to make the opening to big or you make the tray weak and then the glass will break easier. The milling can probably be done without taking the glass out of the tray, but you'd have to be really careful, don't cut the plastic quite all the way thru with the power tool, and protect the glass from being scratched. After that you'd have to figure out the lighting you want and how to mount and aim it. If you used a lightbulb you'd only want it as a turn signal. you would not want it to be also a brake light. ((I'd be worried as a brake light a light bulb might heat the glass unevenly and crack it. It all depends on exactly how the bulb is mounted.))||||reg||1
Z||000040||Mark||02-11-2000||06:34 PM||||As I said before, you must use a resistor to limit the forward current in an LED.  If you don't, the only resistance that limits current is the forward resistance of the LED itself, which is very low. This is particularly important if the supply voltage varies significantly (as it would in an automotive application).  ||||reg||1
